    "https://njit.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/NJIT_Careers/job/Fenster-Hall/Associate-Director-for-Institutional-Research_JR100316-1": {
      "title": "Associate Director for Institutional Research",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAre you a current NJIT employee? Apply here through the Internal Jobs Hub.     Title: Associate Director for Institutional Research     Department: Institutional Effectiveness      Reports To: Exec Dir, Institutional Effect        Position Summary: 1. Manage and oversee SQL Server database systems, including database administration, ETL pipelines from Oracle (Banner), and Snowflake integration, to ensure validated, secure, and accessible datasets. 2. Conduct statistical analysis and institutional research studies on enrollment, retention, graduation, and post-graduation outcomes to inform institutional planning and effectiveness. 3. Direct the preparation and submission of external surveys and benchmarking reports (e.g., IPEDS, US News, ASEE, HERD, state systems), ensuring accuracy and consistency. 4. Produce economic impact reports for research projects and for the university, including analysis of direct, indirect, and induced economic contributions 5. Maintain official Factbook dashboards, ensuring accuracy, timeliness, and accessibility for institutional stakeholders. 6. Design and implement AI innovations in Institutional Effectiveness, including the development of an institutional chatbot to improve knowledge management and access to reports and documentation. 7. Develop and maintain automated workflows and reporting process, improving efficiency and accuracy across IR functions. 8. Apply principles of system theory and practice to evaluate institutional processes and recommend strategies for improving institutional effectiveness. 9. Create and maintain dynamics dashboards, reports, and data visualizations for leadership and campus stakeholders. 10. Support assessment and accreditation activities by aligning reporting and data analyses with institutional standards. 11. Contribute to the professional advancement of Institutional Research by engaging with national organizations (e.g., AIR, NJAIR) through committee service, publications, and presentations. 12. Provide quality control reviews of institutional reports and supervise analysts, interns, or project staff as needed. 13. Prepare and validate data for federal and state reporting systems, including the Integrated Postsecondary Education Data System (IPEDS), National Science Foundation (NSF) surveys including HERD, U.S. Department of Education reporting, NJ SURE submissions, and NCAA reporting, ensuring compliance with regulatory definitions and reporting standards. 14. Support data analysis or documentation for institutional or programmatic accreditation processes, work with standards aligned with Middle States Commission on Higher Education (MSCHE), ABET, AACSB, or the National Architectural Accrediting Board (NAAB). 15. Produce or support data submissions for college ranking and benchmarking organizations, including U.S. News &amp; World Report, Times Higher Education World University Rankings, Peterson&#39;s, and the Common Data Set. 16. Design, implement, or support enterprise data systems integration, including relational databases, ETL processes, and cross-platform data architecture. 17. Develop and maintain data visualizations, dynamic dashboards, or decision-support tools for organizational leadership. 18. Design and implement automated data pipelines, workflow, and build institutional-wide AI tool to enhance organizational knowledge management, information access, and operational efficiency. 19. Work with the National Center for Education Statistics and federally compliant secure research environments, including identity management systems, role-based access controls, and encrypted workspaces to access and analyze restricted-use or confidential data in accordance with federal privacy and security standards. 20. Produce economic impact analyses and applied quantitative research reports for institutional, governmental, or community stakeholders. 21. Perform the foregoing using higher education enterprise systems including Oracle Banner or comparable ERP systems preferred and cloud-based or enterprise data platforms including Snowflake and AWS.   EDUCATION AND EXPERIENCE REQUIREMENT: Requires a Master’s degree in Financial Economics and 2 years of experience in job offered or 2 years of experience in the Related Occupation.  IMPLAN Certified Economist certification required.  RELATED OCCUPATION: Institutional Research Data Analyst or any other job title performing the following job duties: 1. Conduct statistical analysis and institutional research studies on enrollment, retention, graduation, and post-graduation outcomes to inform institutional planning and effectiveness. 2. Develop and maintain automated workflows and reporting process, improving efficiency and accuracy across IR functions. 3. Apply principles of system theory and practice to evaluate institutional processes and recommend strategies for improving institutional effectiveness. 4. Create and maintain dynamics dashboards, reports, and data visualizations for leadership and campus stakeholders. 5. Support assessment and accreditation activities by aligning reporting and data analyses with institutional standards. 6. Contribute to the professional advancement of Institutional Research by engaging with national organizations (e.g., AIR, NJAIR) through committee service, publications, and presentations. 7. Produce or support data submissions for college ranking and benchmarking organizations, including U.S. News &amp; World Report, Times Higher Education World University Rankings, Peterson&#39;s, and the Common Data Set. 8. Develop and maintain data visualizations, dynamic dashboards, or decision-support tools for organizational leadership. 9. Produce economic impact analyses and applied quantitative research reports for institutional, governmental, or community stakeholders. SALARY RANGE: $90,272.00 to $144,786.96.00/year At the university's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be exempted where the candidate can demonstrate to the satisfaction of the university an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position.            Union: Professional Staff Association (PSA)            Range: Professional Staff -27            Compensation: $75,560.00 - $141,948.00            NJIT considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. This pay range represents base pay only and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, or other items.   FLSA: Exempt            Time Type: Full time            Pay Rate: Salary            Additional Information: As an EEO employer NJIT is committed to building a diverse and inclusive teaching, research, and working environment and strongly encourages applications from individuals with disabilities, minorities, veterans, and women. Employment at NJIT is subject to the provisions of New Jersey First Act which mandates new employees, who are not NJ residents, to establish primary residence in New Jersey within one year of their appointment to certain positions. The law does not apply to any individual employed at NJIT on a temporary or per semester basis as a visiting or adjunct professor, teacher, lecturer, researcher or administrator. For more information on the act please click here. If special accommodations are needed in applying for a position, please visit the Department of Human Resources located in Fenster Hall, 5th Floor, University Heights, Newark, NJ 07102 or call (973) 596-3140. If you have questions, please email the Human Resources Department at hr@njit.edu. Information regarding NJIT campus security, personal safety, and fire safety including topics such as, disciplinary procedures, crime prevention, NJIT Police law enforcement authority, crime reporting policies, and crime statistics for the most recent three year period is available on the NJIT Department of Public Safety here . NJIT is an E-Verify employer and uses E-Verify to confirm work authorization of each new hire."
    },
    "https://njit.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/NJIT_Careers/job/Central-King-Building/Associate-Dean-of-Students-for-Community-Standards-and-Academic-Integrity_JR100080": {
      "title": "Associate Dean of Students for Community Standards and Academic Integrity",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAre you a current NJIT employee? Apply here through the Internal Jobs Hub.     Title: Associate Dean of Students for Community Standards and Academic Integrity     Department: Student Affairs & Dean of Students      Reports To: Senior Vice President of Student Affairs and Dean of Students        Position Summary: Working with supervision from the VPSADOS, the Associate Dean is responsible for supporting campus life and campus climate, the development and academic progress of students, and retention initiatives. The Associate Dean is also primarily responsible for the processing and adjudication of the Student Code of Conduct and Academic Integrity cases for students. This position works closely with academic departments and serves on several campus wide committees. Essential Functions Investigates and adjudicates student conduct and academic integrity violations. Investigate complaints by interviewing involved parties. Decides the appropriate course of action and prepares an investigation report and statement of charges; informs and advises the accused, without bias, of charges, evidence, and procedures. Prepares case material for the Student Conduct Board and presents the case before the committee. Follow up to ensure that the appropriate correspondence or records have been written or created. Recruits, trains, supervises, and develops the Hearing and Development Officer. Regularly assesses the effectiveness of the program; develops and facilitates regular presentations and workshops for the campus community. Oversees the recruitment, training, and evaluation of all Hearing Board members and Conduct Advisors. Reviews and develops policies and procedures impacting students' behavior within and outside the classroom. Identifies policies and procedures within student services that need to be revised or replaced. Works with faculty, administrative, and student groups to recommend or approve changes and publishes new policies and procedures in student handbooks or other media. Provides data analysis, conducts advising interviews, serves on university committees as a member and/or resource, coordinates the student appeals process, and coordinates staff effort on these activities. Coordinates and provides academic support and advice for students placed on academic probation, suspension, or re-admission. Provides individual assistance to students to support academic improvement, progress toward graduation, and the university's educational mission. Serves as a liaison between students and college officials and faculty members in resolving academic and personal concerns. Conducts individual interviews with students, faculty, staff, parents, and others; provides follow-up correspondence; and makes final decisions to uphold or accept policies carried out by other offices. Studies, initiates, and coordinates university wide efforts to increase student retention. Monitors campus retention issues and trends, making adjustments on an ongoing basis. Ensures compliance with FERPA for student conduct records contained in the Office of the Dean of Students, the Office of Student Involvement and Leadership (OSIL), and the Office of Residence Life by supervising the maintenance, security, and proper disclosure of such records. Provides certification for agencies requesting student conduct information, in accordance with FERPA. Additional Functions Acts as a Title IX Hearing Administrator or Investigator. Shares responsibility with the Senior Associate Dean to represent the Dean when designated. Assists with supervising departments reporting to the Office of the Dean of Students, specifically the Office of Residence Life. Performs other duties as assigned. Prerequisite Qualifications A master's degree in Student Personnel Administration, Higher Education Administration, Counseling, or a related field is required; a doctorate is preferred. Seven years of experience in problem-solving in a university setting, demonstrating progressively increased student and administrative responsibilities, particularly in the area of academic misconduct adjudication and related processes. Five years of experience supervising full-time staff. Demonstrated experience investigating and managing student conduct and academic integrity cases, including appeals. Demonstrated understanding of artificial intelligence (AI) technologies and their impact on higher education, including academic integrity, student conduct processes, student support services, and administrative operations. Ability to evaluate emerging AI-related issues, interpret AI-generated evidence appropriately, and contribute to the development of policies, procedures, and educational initiatives related to the responsible use of AI. Demonstrated effectiveness as a culturally competent professional with broad experience interacting with and supporting the needs of a diverse student population. A high level of collegiality in working with other student affairs units as well as campus and community constituencies, along with the ability to build collaborative relationships characterized by mutual respect, trust, and commitment to shared goals. Highly effective in all aspects of crisis management, including the ability to collaborate with others in managing crises. Demonstrated experience as a manager of complex organizational processes; demonstrated ability to analyze data to drive decisions. Effective in setting priorities and deadlines to ensure project goals and objectives are met. Demonstrated problem-solving and time management skills, and the ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing. Must be flexible in work hours and able to work some nights and weekends.   Preferred Qualifications A doctorate is preferred. Demonstrated knowledge of Title IX; previous experience investigating, adjudicating, and/or advising Title IX complaints; and/or experience with outreach and prevention efforts. Proficiency with the Maxient conduct tracking database is also preferred. Experience developing policies, procedures, educational programs, or operational practices related to the use of artificial intelligence in higher education. Compensation: $115,000 - $135,000   . At the university's discretion, the education and experience prerequisites may be exempted where the candidate can demonstrate to the satisfaction of the university an equivalent combination of education and experience specifically preparing the candidate for success in the position.            Range: Non-Aligned -8            NJIT considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate's work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as, market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. This pay range represents base pay only and excludes any additional items such as incentives, bonuses, or other items.     FLSA: Exempt            Time Type: Full time            Pay Rate: Salary            Additional Information: As an EEO employer NJIT is committed to building a diverse and inclusive teaching, research, and working environment and strongly encourages applications from individuals with disabilities, minorities, veterans, and women. Employment at NJIT is subject to the provisions of New Jersey First Act which mandates new employees, who are not NJ residents, to establish primary residence in New Jersey within one year of their appointment to certain positions. The law does not apply to any individual employed at NJIT on a temporary or per semester basis as a visiting or adjunct professor, teacher, lecturer, researcher or administrator. For more information on the act please click here. If special accommodations are needed in applying for a position, please visit the Department of Human Resources located in Fenster Hall, 5th Floor, University Heights, Newark, NJ 07102 or call (973) 596-3140. If you have questions, please email the Human Resources Department at hr@njit.edu. Information regarding NJIT campus security, personal safety, and fire safety including topics such as, disciplinary procedures, crime prevention, NJIT Police law enforcement authority, crime reporting policies, and crime statistics for the most recent three year period is available on the NJIT Department of Public Safety here . NJIT is an E-Verify employer and uses E-Verify to confirm work authorization of each new hire."
    },
    "https://njit.wd108.myworkdayjobs.com/NJIT_Careers/job/Adjunct-Instructor-in-Chemistry_JR100114-1": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Chemistry",
